.TH CERTIFY 8
.SH NAME
certify \- create and inspect authentication certificates
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B certify
[
.B \-f
]
.B init
[
.I owner
]
.br
.B certify
.B user
.I name
[
.I file
]
.br
.B certify firstboot
.br
.B certify show
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Certify
makes the two files that Inferno's mutual authentication needs: a
signer key for the machine, and one certificate per user.
.PP
The authentication protocol
.RB ( keyring-auth (2))
has each side send a certificate signed by a common signer, and prove
possession of the corresponding secret key.  It does not require the
signer to be on line: the signer's
.I public
key travels inside every certificate as
.BR spk ,
and each side verifies the other's certificate with it.
.I Certify
therefore needs no
.IR keyfs (4),
.IR signer (8)
or
.IR logind (8)
daemon.
.SS Init
.B certify init
creates
.B /keydb/signerkey
for
.IR owner
(default
.BR signer ).
The file holds the signer's private key and must be readable only by
root.  An existing key is never replaced: replacing it invalidates
every certificate already issued.
.SS User
.B certify user
.I name
writes a certificate for
.I name
to
.IR file ,
by default
.BR /usr/\fIname\fP/keyring/default ,
creating the directory if necessary.
Each run generates a fresh RSA key pair; the certificate signs its
public half.  Running
.B certify user
again therefore makes a
.I different
certificate, and the one it replaces stays valid, because there is no
revocation: see
.SM BUGS .
.PP
.B \-f
allows an existing certificate to be replaced.
.PP
Give the file to the user; it is what
.I listen
reads on the server and what
.IR rcmd (1)
or
.IR cpu (1)
present on the client.  With
.IR rcmd ,
.B \-f
.IR file
selects it.
.SS Firstboot
.B certify firstboot
is what the PC kernel's init runs before starting
.IR svcd (8):
create the signer key if it is missing, then create a certificate for
the console user (the name in
.BR /dev/user )
if that is missing too.  This makes a freshly provisioned machine
usable without an operator, at the cost of a key that differs from
machine to machine.
.SS Show
.B certify show
prints the signer key and every
.B /usr/*/keyring/default
it can find.
.SH SECURITY
The protocol is mutual: each side sends a certificate and proves
possession of the matching secret key, and the user name is part of the
signed public key, so a certificate cannot be relabelled.  That much was
verified.
.PP
What it is not:
.IP \(bu
A certificate is a
.I bearer token.
Its file also holds the private key.  Anyone who can read it can act as
that user.
.IP \(bu
One signer key per machine, never expiring, with no revocation list.
Replacing
.B /keydb/signerkey
invalidates every certificate; replacing one user's certificate does
not invalidate the one that user already has.
.IP \(bu
There are no real user identifiers inside an Inferno instance.
.B runas
writes
.BR /dev/user ,
and any process can write it.  Every process on the machine can also
read
.B /keydb/signerkey
and every certificate.  Treat the user name as accounting, not as a
security boundary; the boundary is the
.I cpud
allowlist.
.IP \(bu
.B \-a rc4_128
uses Inferno's SSL device, which is the SSLv2-era record layer with RC4
and MD5.  It stops casual sniffing on a LAN; it is not modern
confidentiality.  With
.B \-a none
the session is authenticated but completely in the clear, including the
certificate exchange.
.IP \(bu
The machine key created by
.B firstboot
is written into the root file system.  Clone an image
.I before
first boot, or every clone shares one signer key.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create the machine key and a certificate for the console user:
.IP
.EX
; certify firstboot
certify: signer key for signer in /keydb/signerkey
certify: inferno -> /usr/inferno/keyring/default
.EE
.PP
Add a user, then run a command as that user:
.IP
.EX
; certify user alice
certify: alice -> /usr/alice/keyring/default
; rcmd -f /usr/alice/keyring/default -e rc4_128 tcp!server!6668 echo hello
hello
.EE
.PP
The server side needs the same command started through
.IR listen (1)
with authentication enabled, for example:
.IP
.EX
listen -a rc4_128 tcp!*!6668 /dis/sh.dis /lib/cpusession.sh
.EE
.PP
where
.B /lib/cpusession.sh
runs
.BI "runas $user /dis/cpud.dis" ;
.I listen
sets
.B $user
from the certificate the client presented.
.SH FILES
.TF /usr/\fIname\fP/keyring/default
.TP
.B /keydb/signerkey
signer private key; root only
.TP
.BI /usr/ name /keyring/default
user certificate
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/auth/certify.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR listen (1),
.IR rcmd (1),
.IR cpu (1),
.IR cpud (8),
.IR keyring-auth (2),
.IR createsignerkey (8)
.SH BUGS
To withdraw a user, delete the certificate file; that only helps if the
user has not kept a copy.  To withdraw everyone, replace
.B /keydb/signerkey
and reissue every certificate.  Nothing expires on its own.
.PP
See
.SM SECURITY
for what the mechanism does not provide.
